    <h2 class="article-title">Of Covenants and Commitments</h2>
    <div class="article-body">
        <p><span data-span="s1" data-article="A11" data-type="concept" data-named="false"><strong>We are</strong> children of God by birth</span>. <span data-span="s2" data-article="A11" data-type="concept" data-named="false"><strong>We become</strong> disciples of Christ by covenant</span>. <span data-span="s3" data-article="A11" data-type="direct" data-named="false">Jesus said plainly: "If you love me, keep my commandments," and again "You are my friends if you do what I command."</span> <span data-span="s4" data-article="A11" data-type="concept" data-named="false">This does not mean that we save ourselves through perfect obedience and good works. Rather, obedience is an expression of love.</span></p>
        <p><span data-span="s5" data-article="A11" data-type="concept" data-named="false">Recognizing that the discipline required to be a "disciple" is not automatic, and it is not easy, <strong>we labor to surrender</strong> our will into His and then live it. Not merely profess it. Not merely believe it. Live it — in the body, in the home, in the world, every day of the week, as reality.</span> <span data-span="s19" data-article="A11" data-type="concept" data-named="false">No soul finishes in a day, and He is patient with our becoming.</span> <span data-span="s6" data-article="A11" data-type="direct" data-named="false">Jesus called together the multitude and said, "If any man will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross daily, and follow me."</span> We hope to live up to the Lord's expectations. <span data-span="s7" data-article="A11" data-type="concept" data-named="false"><strong>We embrace</strong> His name as our central identity, staking our lives on Him</span>. <span data-span="s8" data-article="A11" data-type="concept" data-named="false">Whenever we sin and fall short of the glory of God, we repent sincerely and strive to sin no more.</span></p>{% if aoidSecondEdition.on %}
        <p><span data-span="s20" data-article="A11" data-type="concept" data-named="false">Christ's disciples today are not all of one mind on sexual holiness, yet the word of God on it is as plain now as it was then.</span> <span data-span="s21" data-article="A11" data-type="paraphrase" data-named="false">In the body, <strong>we receive</strong> sexual union as a holy gift whose rightful place is marriage, the union of a man and a woman who become one flesh, and outside marriage we live chastely.</span><sup><a href="#a11-fn" id="a11-fnref" class="article-fnmark" aria-label="Footnote">†</a></sup></p>
        <p><span data-span="s22" data-article="A11" data-type="paraphrase" data-named="false">In the home, <strong>we hold</strong> marriage in honor among all, for what God hath joined together, let not man put asunder.</span></p>
        <p><span data-span="s23" data-article="A11" data-type="paraphrase" data-named="false">In the world, though not of the world, <strong>we render</strong> to Caesar the things that are Caesar's, and to God the things that are God's. We honor those in authority and pray for them; where their commands contradict God's, we obey God rather than men.</span> <span data-span="s24" data-article="A11" data-type="paraphrase" data-named="false"><strong>We work</strong> with our own hands, as we are able, that we may eat our own bread and have something to give to those in need.</span></p>{% endif %}
        <p><span data-span="s9" data-article="A11" data-type="paraphrase" data-named="false"><strong>We accept</strong> that Christ chose to be baptized to fulfill all righteousness, and instituted ordinances to remember Him always.</span>{% if aoidSecondEdition.on %} <span data-span="s25" data-article="A11" data-type="direct" data-named="false">On the night He was betrayed, He took bread and the cup and said, "This do in remembrance of me," and as often as His disciples eat and drink it, they show the Lord's death till He come.</span>{% endif %} <span data-span="s10" data-article="A11" data-type="concept" data-named="false">Each religious institution formalizes these ordinances and sacraments for their own membership. Still, the Lord receives all who sincerely come to Him in His name and denies none — black and white, bond and free, male and female; He remembereth the heathen, and to Him all are alike, both Jew and Gentile.</span></p>
        <p><strong>We recognize</strong> that <span data-span="s11" data-article="A11" data-type="direct" data-named="false">His apostles were sent into the world and taught, "Be ye doers of the word, and not hearers only."</span> <span data-span="s12" data-article="A11" data-type="direct" data-named="false">Those who feel the Spirit of Truth in their teachings and begin to believe in Christ always ask, "What shall we do?"</span> <span data-span="s13" data-article="A11" data-type="direct" data-named="false">The answer was as plain then as it is now: "Repent and be baptized in the name of Jesus Christ for the remission of sins, and ye shall receive the gift of the Holy Ghost."</span> <span data-span="s14" data-article="A11" data-type="paraphrase" data-named="false">Each disciple must feel after God's Spirit for what it means to be "born of the water and the Spirit" and how to enter into covenants with Christ.</span></p>
        <p><span data-span="s15" data-article="A11" data-type="paraphrase" data-named="false"><strong>We walk</strong> the path Christ set before us — Himself being the way, the truth, and the life.</span> <span data-span="s16" data-article="A11" data-type="direct" data-named="false"><strong>We pray</strong> always and "faint not."</span> <span data-span="s17" data-article="A11" data-type="concept" data-named="false"><strong>We endure</strong> in faith through joy and trials as God refines our new identity into mature discipleship over a lifetime of spiritual sanctification by the power of His Spirit.</span> <span data-span="s18" data-article="A11" data-type="concept" data-named="false"><strong>We seek</strong> to enter into God's Kingdom, which is at hand — now, in this moment — for those who have eyes to see, ears to hear, and willing hearts.</span></p>
        <div class="article-rjw">
            <p class="rjw-prompt">What covenant or commitment do you most readily profess — and find hardest to actually live?</p>
            <textarea class="rjw-textarea" data-rjw-key="rjw-A11" placeholder="Your reflection (optional)" rows="3" aria-label="Reflect on Of Covenants and Commitments"></textarea>
        </div>{% if aoidSecondEdition.on %}
        <p class="article-footnote" id="a11-fn">† This teaching is set down as biblically anchored witness rather than as common ground. Of sixteen Christian traditions, measured by their own adopted texts, eleven teach that sexual relations belong within marriage, and ten that marriage is between a man and a woman; four are divided within themselves on the first and five on the second, and one holds no common position. That falls short of the more-than-four-in-five standard these Articles keep. See <a href="/aoid/disclosures/sexual-holiness" class="article-companion-link"><em>Of Sexual Holiness: A Disclosure of Division</em></a>. <a href="#a11-fnref" aria-label="Back to the text">↩</a></p>{% endif %}
    </div>